This is the **Erdős–Szemerédi sum-product conjecture**, a well-known open problem in additive combinatorics. The problem asks whether for every finite set of integers $A$ and every $\epsilon > 0$,

$$\max(|A+A|,\, |A\cdot A|) \gg_\epsilon |A|^{2-\epsilon}.$$

**Status of the problem.** This is genuinely open. The conjecture dates back to Erdős and Szemerédi (1983). The best known partial results give weaker exponents:

- Erdős–Szemerédi originally proved a bound of the form $\max(|A+A|,|A\cdot A|) \gg |A|^{1+c}$ for some small absolute constant $c > 0$.
- Solymosi (2009) proved $\max(|A+A|,|A\cdot A|) \gg |A|^{4/3}/\log|A|$ for sets of real numbers (hence integers).
- Konyagin and Rudnev (2014) and subsequent work improved constants but the exponent $4/3$ (up to subpolynomial factors) remains essentially the best known for general sets.
- Recent work (e.g., by Roche-Newton, Rudnev, Shkredov, Stevens, de Zeeuw and others) has pushed toward exponents slightly above $4/3$ in restricted settings, but the full conjecture with exponent $2 - \epsilon$ remains far out of reach.

The conjecture is widely believed to be true, and the "correct" exponent is conjectured to be $2 - o(1)$, but no proof is known. The problem is explicitly tagged `@[category research open]` in the formalization, confirming its open status.

Since this is an open research problem with no known proof, I cannot provide a complete proof.

